      "text": "filename: docs/knife-sharpening.md\n---\n# How to Sharpen a Kitchen Knife by Hand\n\n## Gather Your Tools  \nBefore you begin, gather the necessary tools to ensure a safe and effective sharpening process. You will need a high-quality whetstone—preferably a natural or synthetic stone with a medium to fine grit, such as 800 or 1000 grit. A sharpening rod or a bench block provides stability and helps maintain the correct angle. A towel or cloth is essential to protect your work surface and to wipe down the knife after sharpening. Additionally, have a pair of scissors or a knife block nearby to store your knife safely once it's done. A magnifying glass or a small flashlight can help you inspect the edge for any imperfections. Make sure your knife is clean and dry before starting. A sharp knife is safer and more effective in the kitchen, so proper maintenance is key.\n\n## Sharpen the Edge  \nHold the knife at a consistent angle—typically between 15 and 20 degrees—using your dominant hand. This angle is crucial for creating a sharp, durable edge. Place the beveled edge of the knife against the whetstone and move it in a smooth, forward motion, applying even pressure. Use a back-and-forth motion, starting from the heel of the blade and moving toward the tip. Repeat this motion for about 10 to 15 seconds per side. After each pass, lift the knife and wipe the edge with a damp cloth to remove metal particles. Gradually increase the grit of the whetstone if needed, moving from coarse to fine to refine the edge. Always maintain the same angle throughout the process to avoid dulling or damaging the blade. A well-maintained edge will cut through food with precision and reduce the risk of slipping.\n\n## Test and Store  \nAfter sharpening, test the knife’s edge by gently slicing through a piece of paper or a thin apple slice. The blade should glide smoothly without resistance. If the edge feels dull or requires more effort, repeat the sharpening process. Once satisfied, clean the knife with warm, soapy water and dry it thoroughly. Store the knife in a knife block or a sheath to prevent damage and maintain its sharpness. Regular sharpening not only extends the life of your knife but also improves your cooking experience. Remember, a properly sharpened knife is a safer tool and a more efficient one. Always use a whetstone and maintain the correct angle to achieve optimal results. With consistent care, your kitchen knives will remain sharp and ready for use.",
